Semaphore
A semaphore controls access to a shared resource through the use of a counter (permit count).
If the counter is greater than zero, then access is allowed. If it is zero,
then access is denied, and any waiting threads will be blocked until a permit can be acquired.
To access the resource, a thread must be granted a permit from the semaphore.
------
Semaphore
Semaphore has two constructors:
Semaphore(int permits)
Semaphore(int permits, boolean fair)

Here, permits is specified by the permit count — the number of permits available for acquiring.
If fair is true, then the Semaphore will guarantee first-in-first-out granting of permits,
 ensuring that the longest waiting thread gets permits.
If fair is false, then the order of thread acquisition is not guaranteed to be fair.
---------
Semaphore
To release a permit, call the release method, which has two forms:

void release()
void release(int permits)

1. void release()
This method is used to release a single permit back to the semaphore.
It increases the number of available permits by 1.

If other threads are waiting to acquire permits, this may unblock one of them.

2. void release(int permits)
This method is used to release a specified number of permits back to the semaphore.

It increases the number of available permits by the specified amount.

If other threads are waiting to acquire permits, this may unblock multiple threads depending on the number of permits released.
